hypotheses.statement:
For the algebra in Equation <a href="#eq:quotient-algebra" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:quotient-algebra">[eq:quotient-algebra]</a>, over every commutative ring $S$ with identity, $$\label{eq:trace-formula}
 \operatorname{tr}(M_v)=\mathcal L(vJ)
 \qquad(v\in S[x]).$$

Suppose that the coefficients of the relations in Equation <a href="#eq:quotient-algebra" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:quotient-algebra">[eq:quotient-algebra]</a> and of $v$ lie in $\mathbb C$, and write $Q(y)=\det(yI-M_v)$. Every common zero $\xi\in\mathbb C^n$ of the $f_i$ satisfies $Q(v(\xi))=0$. If all coefficients are real and $\eta$ is a simple real root of $Q$, there is a common zero $\xi\in\mathbb R^n$ with $v(\xi)=\eta$. Moreover, for any polynomial $g$ over $\mathbb C$, $$\label{eq:composition-determinant}
 M_{g(v)}=g(M_v),\qquad
 \det M_{g(v)}=\prod_{Q(\alpha)=0}g(\alpha),$$ where the roots in the product are counted with multiplicity.

Let $n\ge1$ be polynomially bounded and polynomial-time computable, and let $\ell\ge2$ be a short polynomial-time computable integer. Suppose that $r_1,\ldots,r_n,v$ are controlled integer polynomial families in variables $x=(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$ and a polynomially bounded number of parameter variables $t$, uniformly in $i$, and that $\deg_x r_i<\ell$. In the monomial basis of $$\mathbb Z[t,x]/(x_1^\ell-r_1,\ldots,x_n^\ell-r_n),$$ let $M_v$ denote multiplication by $v$. Then $$\label{eq:controlled-characteristic}
 Q(t,y)=\det(yI-M_v)$$ is a controlled family, monic of $y$-degree $D=\ell^n$. If $d$ and $2^b$ are common total-degree and coefficient-norm bounds on the $r_i,v$, and $T=d+n(\ell-1)$, valid total-degree and log-norm bounds on $Q$ are $$\label{eq:determinant-size-bounds}
 d_Q=D\bigl(1+d(1+T)\bigr),\qquad
 b_Q=D^2+D\bigl(1+b(1+T)\bigr).$$ The construction is compatible with specializing any parameters.

For integers $B\ge1$ and $s\ge1$, define $$\begin{aligned}
 C_{B,s}(z)
   &=\prod_{k=-B}^{B}
       \bigl(2^{\max(0,k)}+2^{\max(0,-k)}z\bigr)^{2s},\\
 A_{B,s}(z)
   &=\frac{C_{B,s}(z)-C_{B,s}(-z)}
           {C_{B,s}(z)+C_{B,s}(-z)}.
\end{aligned}$$ The denominator is at least $1$ for every real $z$, and $A_{B,s}(0)=0$. For $2^{-B}\le|z|\le2^B$, $$|A_{B,s}(z)-\mathop{\mathrm{sgn}}z|\le2\cdot3^{-2s}.$$ Moreover $C_{B,s}$ is an integer polynomial with $$\deg C_{B,s}=2s(2B+1),\qquad
 \left\lVert C_{B,s}\right\rVert_1\le2^{2s(2B+1)(B+1)}.$$

For the nonnegative polynomial $F$ and deformation in Equation <a href="#eq:existential-deformation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:existential-deformation">[eq:existential-deformation]</a>, the minimum $$m(u)=\min_{x\in\mathbb R^n}h_u(x)$$ exists for every real $u\ge0$. The function $m$ is continuous and nondecreasing, and $Q(u,m(u))=0$. Furthermore, $$F\text{ has a real zero}
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 m(u)\text{ remains bounded as }u\to+\infty.$$

The polynomial $Q(0,y)$ has $D$ distinct complex roots. If $\left\lVert Q\right\rVert_1\le2^H$, where $H\ge0$ is an integer, there is a nonzero polynomial $\Delta\in\mathbb Z[u]$ such that every parameter where $Q(u,y)$ has a repeated complex root is a zero of $\Delta$, and $$\label{eq:existential-discriminant-bound}
 \left\lVert\Delta\right\rVert_1\le2^{H_\Delta},\qquad
 H_\Delta=(2D)^2+2D(H+D+1).$$

Let $F\in\mathbb Z[x_1,\ldots,x_n]$ be nonnegative on $\mathbb R^n$, let $n\ge1$, and choose an odd $\ell\ge3$ with $\ell>\deg F$. Define $G,h_u,f_i,Q,D$ by Equations <a href="#eq:existential-deformation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:existential-deformation">[eq:existential-deformation]</a> and <a href="#eq:existential-characteristic" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:existential-characteristic">[eq:existential-characteristic]</a>. Let $H,V$ be nonnegative integers bounding $\log_2\left\lVert Q\right\rVert_1$ and $\deg_uQ$, respectively, and choose the parameters in Equation <a href="#eq:existential-specialization-parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:existential-specialization-parameters">[eq:existential-specialization-parameters]</a>. Then $$q(y)=Q(U,y)$$ is monic and squarefree, has degree $D$ and coefficient norm at most $2^K$, and satisfies $$\label{eq:existential-root-equivalence}
 F\text{ has a real zero}
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 q\text{ has a real root less than }R.$$ Every real root of $q$ is the value of $h_U$ at a real common zero of $f_1(\cdot,U),\ldots,f_n(\cdot,U)$.*

*When $F$ varies in a controlled family, $\ell$ is chosen from its degree bound as above, and $H,V$ are polynomial-time computable short bounds, $q$ is controlled. All remaining bound parameters in Equation <a href="#eq:existential-specialization-parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:existential-specialization-parameters">[eq:existential-specialization-parameters]</a>, including $D,H_\Delta$, are computable in polynomial time and have polynomial binary length.

Let $W\in\mathbb R[c_1,\ldots,c_k]$ be nonnegative on $\mathbb R^k$, where $k\ge1$, and let $C$ be a nonempty subset of $Z=\{W=0\}$ that is both open and closed in $Z$. Choose an odd integer $\ell\ge3$ with $\ell>\deg W$, and put $$G(c)=\sum_{i=1}^k c_i^{\ell+1},\qquad
 H_w(c)=G(c)+(\ell+1)wW(c).$$ In the quotient by $$\label{eq:candidate-relations}
 c_i^\ell+w\frac{\partial W}{\partial c_i}=0
 \qquad(1\le i\le k),$$ form the coordinate multiplication polynomials $$\label{eq:coordinate-polynomials}
 P_i(w,T)=\det(TI-M_{c_i})=\sum_j P_{i,j}(T)w^j.$$ For each $i$, let $j_i$ be the largest index for which $P_{i,j_i}\ne0$. There exists $c^*\in C$ such that $$P_{i,j_i}(c_i^*)=0\qquad(1\le i\le k).$$ If $W$ is a controlled family of integer polynomials, $k$ is polynomially bounded, and $\ell$ is short and computable in polynomial time, then the polynomials $P_i$ and $P_{i,j}$ are uniformly controlled.

